title: Shai-Hulud npm Worm
page: breaches/shai-hulud-npm-worm.html
as-of: 2026-11-22
promise: Beginning September 14, 2025, Shai-Hulud spread itself across more than 500 npm packages with no attacker involvement after the first push; how it began is disputed.

== At a glance
layout: stats
stat: 500+ | packages compromised | starting with 40+ across several maintainers
stat: 20 | packages per maintainer, at most | re-published automatically by the worm
stat: 2M+ | weekly downloads | of @ctrl/tinycolor, among the first packages hit
TruffleHog, a defender's tool, was run against the developer. The worm returned as Shai-Hulud 2.0 in November 2025, then as Mini Shai-Hulud in 2026.
notes:
Start with the three figures the page leads with.

More than 500 npm packages were compromised, starting with more than 40 across several maintainers. npm is the default package registry for JavaScript and Node.js.

Up to 20 is the number of packages the worm re-published for each compromised maintainer, automatically. The page calls Shai-Hulud the first genuine worm in the npm ecosystem: malware that spread from maintainer to maintainer with no attacker involvement after the first push.

The third figure is the reach of a single package. @ctrl/tinycolor, among the first packages hit, serves over two million downloads a week.

The caption carries the page's two other headline points. TruffleHog, a tool built for defenders, was run against the developer. And the worm returned as Shai-Hulud 2.0 in November 2025, then as Mini Shai-Hulud in 2026.

== What happened, and when
layout: timeline
event: September 14, 2025 | Shai-Hulud begins; the page calls it the first genuine npm worm
event: September 23, 2025 | CISA issues an alert on the npm ecosystem compromise
event: November 2025 | The worm returns as Shai-Hulud 2.0
event: 2026 | The technique appears again in Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack
Holding a maintainer's publish token, it harvested secrets on the developer's machine, republished itself into up to 20 of the maintainer's packages, and committed the secrets to a public repo in the victim's account. Spacing is not to scale.
notes:
The timeline shows the dates the page gives for the incident and its descendants.

Shai-Hulud began on September 14, 2025. Once it held a maintainer's publish token, a bundled payload running as a postinstall script downloaded TruffleHog and turned it on the developer's own machine. A postinstall script is a command a package asks npm to run right after it is installed. Then the worm authenticated to npm as that maintainer, queried the registry for up to twenty other packages they owned, injected itself into each, and force-published. It also created a public GitHub repository named Shai-Hulud in the victim's own account and committed the stolen secrets into it.

CISA issued an alert on September 23, 2025. In November 2025 the worm returned as Shai-Hulud 2.0, and in 2026 the technique appeared again in Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack. Stage 07 covers both.

The page also mentions the late-August 2025 s1ngularity/Nx compromise and the phishing of the debug and chalk maintainers earlier in September 2025. Both come up in the dispute over how this began. The markers are evenly spaced, so read them as order only.

== How it began is disputed
layout: table
row: Who | What the page reports
row: Unit 42 | Said it may have started with phishing spoofing npm, leading to fake MFA-update pages
row: The @ctrl/tinycolor maintainer | Wrote: no phishing; his npm token was taken from a shared repo's Actions secret
row: Wiz | Assessed it as directly downstream of s1ngularity/Nx (disclosure: the site's author works at Wiz)
row: CISA | Its alert names no initial access vector
What every account agrees on is the target: a maintainer's npm publishing token.
notes:
The page sets out three published accounts of how the first maintainer tokens were stolen, plus CISA's alert, which names none. It does not choose between them.

Unit 42 said the attack may have started with a credential-harvesting campaign spoofing npm that sent maintainers to fake multi-factor authentication update pages. Keep the words may have.

The maintainer of @ctrl/tinycolor, among the first packages hit, wrote that there was no phishing involved: his npm token was taken from a GitHub Actions secret in a shared repository.

Wiz assessed the campaign as directly downstream of the late-August 2025 s1ngularity/Nx compromise. Say the disclosure aloud as the page gives it: research published by Wiz is one of the sources cited on the page, and the site's author works at Wiz. Every source is linked so you can read it directly.

CISA's alert names no initial access vector.

What every account agrees on, the page says, is the target: a maintainer's npm publishing token. Stage 01 takes the accounts in turn.

== The kill chain on one slide
layout: flow
step: 01 | Initial Access | Tokens stolen, vector disputed | T1566.002
step: 02 | Supply Chain | A postinstall bundle | T1195.002
step: 03 | Credential Access | TruffleHog on the developer | T1552.001
step: 04 | Propagation | Up to 20 per maintainer | T1195.002
step: 05 | Persistence | Secrets leaked on push | T1543
step: 06 | Exfiltration | Public Shai-Hulud repo | T1567.001
step: 07 | Impact | CISA alert, two descendants | T1195
Arrows follow the page's stage order.
notes:
Here is the whole chain on one slide: the page's seven stages, each with its phase and the first ATT&CK technique the page maps to it.

Stage 01 is the theft of maintainer publishing tokens, with the vector disputed. Stage 02 is the trojanized versions, a roughly 3.6MB bundle run by a postinstall script; the page files it under a phase it calls Supply Chain. Stage 03 is TruffleHog, run against the developer's own machine. Stage 04 is what made it a worm: up to 20 of the maintainer's packages, re-published automatically. Stage 05 is a GitHub Actions workflow, planted where the malware could reach a repository, that leaks the secrets context on every push. Stage 06 is the exfiltration, into a public repository in the victim's own account. Stage 07 is the impact: CISA's alert, then two descendants.

The flow shows T1566.002 for stage 01 because it is the first technique the page maps there, beside T1111. The page's phase label marks that stage's vector as disputed.

The arrows follow the page's stage order. The next slides take each stage in turn.

== Tokens were stolen; accounts disagree on how
layout: stage
stage: 01
phase: Initial Access
technique: T1566.002 | Phishing: Spearphishing Link
technique: T1111 | Multi-Factor Authentication Interception
fact: Unit 42 | may have begun with phishing to fake npm MFA-update pages
fact: tinycolor maintainer | no phishing; his token came from a shared repo's Actions secret
fact: Wiz | directly downstream of the s1ngularity/Nx compromise
fact: CISA | its alert names no initial access vector
Every account agrees on the target: a maintainer's npm publishing token.
notes:
The page's phase label marks this stage's vector as disputed. It maps the stage to T1566.002, Phishing: Spearphishing Link, and T1111, Multi-Factor Authentication Interception.

Unit 42 said the attack may have started with a campaign spoofing npm that sent maintainers to fake MFA update pages. MFA is multi-factor authentication. The page says that pretext would be well chosen: a message saying npm requires an MFA update is not obviously suspicious, and entering a password and a live one-time code is exactly what the workflow appears to call for. A real-time relay defeats time-based one-time codes, because the attacker stands between the victim and the genuine site and uses the code within its validity window. The same technique had hit the debug and chalk maintainers earlier in September 2025.

Other accounts point elsewhere. The @ctrl/tinycolor maintainer wrote that no phishing was involved, and that his npm token was taken from a GitHub Actions secret in a shared repository. Wiz assessed the campaign as directly downstream of the late-August s1ngularity/Nx compromise. CISA's alert names no initial access vector.

Against the phishing route, the page names phishing-resistant FIDO2 or passkeys, which are origin-bound. The next slide explains that idea in general terms.

== What phishing-resistant MFA means
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Phishing-Resistant MFA | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-phishing-resistant
credit: Also from Microsoft Learn, passkeys (FIDO2) in Microsoft Entra ID (learn.microsoft.com)
Phishing-resistant MFA cannot be relayed or replayed, because the second factor is cryptographically bound to the origin: FIDO2 security keys, passkeys, smartcards. Push prompts, one-time codes and SMS are not phishing-resistant.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and Microsoft Learn, in general terms.

The glossary defines phishing-resistant MFA as multi-factor methods that cannot be relayed or replayed by an attacker, because the second factor is cryptographically bound to the origin. Its examples are FIDO2 security keys, passkeys and smartcards. It says push prompts, one-time codes and SMS are explicitly not phishing-resistant. FIDO2, WebAuthn and passkeys are public-key authentication standards, and passkeys are syncable WebAuthn credentials.

Microsoft Learn explains the binding. A passkey's private key is stored on the user's device and its public key with the site or app. The key pair is unique, so a passkey only works on the site or app it was created for, and every sign-in needs the user present to unlock it. Microsoft calls passkeys almost impossible to phish, and notes that attackers may try downgrade techniques to get around stronger protections like these.

Also from Microsoft Learn, passkeys (FIDO2): https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/entra/identity/authentication/concept-authentication-passkeys-fido2

== Trojanized versions ran a 3.6MB bundle on every install
layout: stage
stage: 02
phase: Supply Chain
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
technique: T1059.007 | JavaScript
fact: First packages | @ctrl/tinycolor (2M+ weekly downloads) and 40+ others
fact: Payload | bundle.js, a roughly 3.6MB minified webpack bundle
fact: Trigger | a postinstall script; 2.0 later moved to preinstall
fact: Cheap check | an order-of-magnitude size jump between patch versions
Installation alone was sufficient. The package never had to be imported or called.
notes:
Stage 02 is the trojanized versions. The page maps it to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain, and T1059.007, JavaScript, and files it under a phase it calls Supply Chain.

The first packages hit included @ctrl/tinycolor, which serves over two million downloads a week, alongside more than forty others spanning several maintainers. The payload was bundle.js, a single minified webpack bundle of roughly 3.6MB, internally modular across reconnaissance, credential harvesting and propagation. Webpack is a tool that packs many JavaScript files into one.

Because it ran as a postinstall script, installing the package was enough; it never had to be imported or called. Shai-Hulud 2.0 later moved to preinstall, a script npm runs before installing the package.

The page treats size as a detection signal in its own right. A colour-manipulation utility does not need a multi-megabyte bundle, and a sudden order-of-magnitude jump in package size between patch versions is, in the page's words, a cheap, high-signal check that almost nobody runs. Eventually 500+ packages were compromised.

The next slide explains, in general terms, how ATT&CK describes this technique.

== What ATT&CK calls compromising the software supply chain
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1195.002 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1195/002/
ATT&CK describes manipulating application software before a final consumer receives it. Its examples include manipulating the source code, manipulating the update or distribution mechanism, and replacing compiled releases with a modified version.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain. Adversaries may manipulate application software prior to receipt by a final consumer, for the purpose of data or system compromise.

It says software supply chain compromise can take place in a number of ways, including manipulation of the application source code, manipulation of the update or distribution mechanism for that software, or replacing compiled releases with a modified version.

The page maps its stages 02 and 04 to this technique, and lists the ATT&CK entry among its primary sources.

== The payload fetched TruffleHog and turned it on the developer's machine
layout: stage
stage: 03
phase: Credential Access
technique: T1552.001 | Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files
technique: T1105 | Ingress Tool Transfer
fact: TruffleHog | a legitimate secret scanner, downloaded and run locally
fact: Harvested | npm tokens, GitHub PATs, AWS, GCP and Azure keys, SSH keys
fact: Why it evades | a legitimate, widely used binary makes a poor signature
fact: Detection angle | unexpected security tooling run by a build or install
The page calls this living off the land, applied to the security toolchain.
notes:
Stage 03 is the credential sweep. The page maps it to T1552.001, Unsecured Credentials: Credentials In Files, and T1105, Ingress Tool Transfer.

TruffleHog exists so defenders can find secrets accidentally committed to their repositories. Shai-Hulud downloaded it and pointed it at the victim's own filesystem and environment.

The page calls this living off the land applied to the security toolchain. It is efficient for the same reason the tool is useful: someone has already done the hard work of enumerating what every cloud provider's credential format looks like and where it tends to be stored. It also frustrates detection, because a widely used open-source binary with a legitimate purpose is a poor signature.

The haul was comprehensive: npm tokens from .npmrc, npm's configuration file; GitHub personal access tokens, or PATs; AWS, GCP and Azure keys; and SSH private keys usable for lateral movement into networks the developer could reach.

The page's detection angle is the unexpected download and execution of security tooling by a build or install process. The next slide explains living off the land in general terms.

== What living off the land means
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, Living Off the Land | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-living-off-the-land
credit: Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1105 (attack.mitre.org)
Living off the land: attackers using legitimate, signed tools already in the environment, such as PowerShell, certutil or the AWS CLI, to evade detection. ATT&CK calls bringing tools in from outside Ingress Tool Transfer.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary and MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

The glossary defines living off the land, LOTL, as attackers using legitimate, signed tools already in the environment, such as PowerShell, certutil and the AWS CLI, to evade detection.

ATT&CK describes a related step under Ingress Tool Transfer. Adversaries may transfer tools or other files from an external system into a compromised environment. ATT&CK says such files may be copied from an adversary-controlled system through the command and control channel, the channel an attacker uses to direct malware, or through alternate protocols such as FTP.

The page maps its stage 03 to T1105, and says Shai-Hulud downloaded TruffleHog to the victim host and ran it locally.

Also from MITRE ATT&CK T1105: https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1105/

== The worm force-published up to 20 of each maintainer's packages
layout: stage
stage: 04
phase: Propagation
technique: T1195.002 | Supply Chain Compromise: Compromise Software Supply Chain
technique: T1078 | Valid Accounts
fact: Mechanism | authenticate to npm as the maintainer with the stolen token
fact: Selection | query the registry for packages they own; take up to 20
fact: Action | inject the payload and force-publish new versions
fact: Attacker per hop | no involvement
Reaching 500+ packages did not require 500 attacker decisions. It required one.
notes:
Stage 04 is the part the page says made it a worm. It maps the stage to T1195.002, Compromise Software Supply Chain, and T1078, Valid Accounts.

Every earlier npm compromise on csoh.org, the page says, required a human at each hop: someone had to steal a token, decide what to publish, and push it. Shai-Hulud removed the human.

Using the token it had just harvested, the malware authenticated to npm as that maintainer, asked the registry which packages they owned, took up to twenty of them, injected its own payload, and published. Each of those packages then infected the next developer who installed it, whose tokens produced the next twenty packages, and so on.

Growth is compounding rather than linear, the page says, and bounded only by how often people run npm install, which in a modern CI-driven ecosystem is constantly. CI is continuous integration: the automation that builds and tests code on every change.

Reaching 500+ packages did not require 500 attacker decisions. It required one.

== A human at every hop, or none
layout: compare
col: Earlier npm compromises on csoh.org | Someone had to steal a token | Someone had to decide what to publish | Someone had to push it
col: Shai-Hulud | Used the token it had just harvested | Asked the registry what the maintainer owned; took up to 20 | Injected its payload and published | The next developer to install it was infected, and so on
Growth is compounding rather than linear, the page says, bounded only by how often people run npm install.
notes:
This slide sets the two models side by side, in the page's words.

For every earlier npm compromise on csoh.org, the page says, a human had to steal a token, decide what to publish, and push it.

Shai-Hulud did those steps itself. With the token it had just harvested, it asked the registry which packages the maintainer owned, took up to twenty, injected its payload and published. Each of those packages infected the next developer who installed it, whose tokens produced the next twenty packages. The page puts attacker involvement per hop at none.

That is why the page calls growth compounding rather than linear, bounded only by how often people run npm install. In a modern CI-driven ecosystem, it says, that is constantly.

== Where it reached a repo, it planted a workflow that leaks secrets
layout: stage
stage: 05
phase: Persistence
technique: T1543 | Create or Modify System Process
technique: T1552.007 | Unsecured Credentials: Container API
fact: Artifact | shai-hulud-workflow.yml, carrying base64-encoded bash
fact: Trigger | push, so it fires for whoever commits next
fact: Expression | ${{ toJSON(secrets) }}: the whole secrets context
fact: Survives cleanup | removing the package leaves the committed workflow file
Any response that stopped at "downgrade the package" left this running.
notes:
Stage 05 is persistence. The page maps it to T1543, Create or Modify System Process, and T1552.007, Container API.

Where the malware could reach a repository, it wrote a GitHub Actions workflow, .github/workflows/shai-hulud-workflow.yml, carrying a base64-encoded bash payload and triggered on push. Base64 is an encoding that writes any data as letters, digits and a few symbols, and anyone can decode it.

The expression ${{ toJSON(secrets) }} serializes the job's entire secrets context in one go, so the workflow did not need to know what any particular repository's secrets were called. It took all of them and shipped them to command and control, the attacker's server.

The page calls this durable in a way the package payload is not. Removing the malicious dependency does nothing to a workflow file sitting in the repository, which will keep firing on every push by whoever commits next.

The page's hunt: search every repository for that filename, and for toJSON(secrets) in any workflow. The next slide explains workflows in general terms.

== What a GitHub Actions workflow is
layout: background
source: How We Use GitHub Actions | https://csoh.org/github-actions.html#what-is-it
credit: Also from the same page's Concepts that bite newcomers (csoh.org)
A workflow is a YAML file under .github/workflows/. GitHub runs it on a fresh virtual machine, the runner, when an event the file lists fires, such as a push. A workflow refers to a secret by name, as secrets.X.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org GitHub Actions page, in general terms.

GitHub Actions is GitHub's built-in automation runner. You drop a YAML file under .github/workflows/, and GitHub watches your repository for the events you list: a push to main, a new pull request, a cron schedule, a manual button click. When a matching event fires, GitHub spins up a fresh virtual machine, the runner, runs the steps you wrote, and tears it down. Every workflow file names its triggers, then one or more jobs, each made of steps.

The same page's concepts section shows a workflow referring to a secret by name, as secrets.X.

The page's stage 05 workflow used toJSON(secrets) instead, which, the page says, serializes the job's entire secrets context, so it did not need to know any secret's name.

Also from the same page's Concepts that bite newcomers: https://csoh.org/github-actions.html#concepts

== Stolen secrets went into a public repo in the victim's own account
layout: stage
stage: 06
phase: Exfiltration
technique: T1567.001 | Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration to Code Repository
fact: Method | GitHub's /user/repos API, with the stolen GitHub token
fact: Repo | public, named "Shai-Hulud", holding the secrets as JSON
fact: Destination | GitHub itself: no attacker infrastructure to blocklist
fact: Some variants | also sent data to a webhook.site endpoint
Public repos turned the theft into a broadcast, readable by anyone who found them.
notes:
Stage 06 is exfiltration. The page maps it to T1567.001, Exfiltration to Code Repository.

Using the stolen GitHub token and GitHub's /user/repos endpoint, the malware created a public repository named Shai-Hulud in the victim's own account and committed the harvested secrets into it as JSON. Shai-Hulud 2.0 used the name Sha1-Hulud: The Second Coming.

As with Nx / s1ngularity three weeks earlier, the page says, the destination is GitHub itself, so there is no attacker infrastructure to blocklist and no unusual egress to notice. Egress is outbound traffic. And as there, the repositories were public, which converted a theft into a broadcast: every secret was readable by anyone who found the repo, including the many automated scrapers that watch GitHub for exactly this.

Some variants also exfiltrated to a webhook.site endpoint, a public service that collects web requests sent to a unique address.

The page reads two independent groups of npm incidents within three weeks settling on the same trick as a pattern rather than a coincidence. Nx / s1ngularity has its own kill chain on csoh.org.

== What ATT&CK calls exfiltration to a code repository
layout: background
source: MITRE ATT&CK T1567.001 | https://attack.mitre.org/techniques/T1567/001/
Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a code repository rather than over their primary command and control channel. Repositories are often reachable through an API over HTTPS, and a popular service already in use can give significant cover.
notes:
Background, from MITRE ATT&CK, in general terms.

ATT&CK files this under Exfiltration Over Web Service: Exfiltration to Code Repository. Adversaries may exfiltrate data to a code repository rather than over their primary command and control channel. Code repositories are often accessible through an API, such as GitHub's, and access to these APIs is often over HTTPS, which ATT&CK says gives the adversary an additional level of protection.

Exfiltration to a code repository can also provide a significant amount of cover to the adversary, ATT&CK says, if it is a popular service already used by hosts within the network.

Command and control, C2, is the channel an attacker uses to direct malware and receive what it collects.

The page maps its stage 06 to this technique.

== CISA alerted on September 23, 2025; the worm then returned twice
layout: stage
stage: 07
phase: Impact
technique: T1195 | Supply Chain Compromise
fact: September 23, 2025 | CISA alert on the npm ecosystem compromise
fact: November 2025 | Shai-Hulud 2.0: preinstall, about 796 packages, 25,000+ repos
fact: 2026 | Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack, which needed no stolen token at all
fact: Trajectory | each generation needed less stolen from the maintainer
The affected population is everyone who installed, not everyone who was targeted.
notes:
Stage 07 is the impact. The page maps it to T1195, Supply Chain Compromise.

CISA, the US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency, published an alert on the npm ecosystem compromise on September 23, 2025. The page calls that the correct signal for a supply-chain event of this shape: the affected population is everyone who installed, not everyone who was targeted.

The page says the lineage since is the reason this entry matters more than its own package count. In November 2025 the worm returned as Shai-Hulud 2.0, or Sha1-Hulud: The Second Coming. It moved execution to preinstall, hit roughly 796 packages, spawned more than 25,000 malicious repositories across about 350 accounts, and added a discussion.yaml workflow that registered infected machines as self-hosted runners for arbitrary command execution. A self-hosted runner is a machine registered to run a repository's workflow jobs, instead of one GitHub provides.

In 2026 the technique appeared again in Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack, which needed no stolen token at all. It poisoned the Actions cache and scraped OIDC from runner memory, publishing packages with valid SLSA provenance. The next slide explains those terms.

== What provenance and SLSA mean
layout: background
source: csoh.org glossary, SLSA | https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-slsa
credit: Also from the csoh.org glossary entries for Provenance and OIDC (csoh.org)
Provenance is a verifiable record of how a build artifact was produced: source commit, builder, dependencies. SLSA is an OpenSSF framework defining levels of build-pipeline integrity, such as signed provenance and isolated builders.
notes:
Background, from the csoh.org glossary, in general terms.

The glossary defines provenance as a verifiable record of how a build artifact was produced: source commit, builder, dependencies.

SLSA, Supply-chain Levels for Software Artifacts, is an OpenSSF framework defining levels of build-pipeline integrity, such as signed provenance and isolated builders.

OIDC, OpenID Connect, is an identity layer on top of OAuth 2.0 that issues ID tokens in the JSON Web Token format.

The page names all three at stage 07, where Mini Shai-Hulud scraped OIDC from runner memory and published packages with valid SLSA provenance. It names them again among its defenses, where SLSA provenance ties a release to a specific workflow run and npm Trusted Publishing uses OIDC.

Also from the csoh.org glossary: https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-provenance and https://csoh.org/glossary.html#term-oidc

== Each generation needed less stolen from the maintainer
layout: compare
col: Shai-Hulud, September 2025 | Started from a maintainer's publish token | Postinstall execution | 500+ packages
col: Shai-Hulud 2.0, November 2025 | Preinstall execution | About 796 packages | Infected machines registered as self-hosted runners
col: Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack, 2026 | No stolen token at all | Actions cache poisoned, OIDC scraped from runner memory | Valid SLSA provenance
The page does not say what 2.0 needed stolen from the maintainer.
notes:
This is the pattern the page draws from the lineage: each generation needed less stolen from the maintainer than the last.

The first Shai-Hulud started from a maintainer's npm publishing token, ran as a postinstall script, and compromised more than 500 packages.

Shai-Hulud 2.0, in November 2025, moved execution to preinstall, hit roughly 796 packages, spawned more than 25,000 malicious repositories across about 350 accounts, and registered infected machines as self-hosted runners through a discussion.yaml workflow. The page does not say what 2.0 needed stolen from the maintainer, so do not fill that in from memory.

Mini Shai-Hulud / TanStack, in 2026, needed no stolen token at all. It poisoned the Actions cache and scraped OIDC from runner memory, and published packages with valid SLSA provenance. It has its own kill chain on csoh.org.

== Six defenses against seven stages
layout: matrix
columns: 01 | 02 | 03 | 04 | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Phishing-resistant MFA for maintainers | 01
row: Install scripts off, dependencies pinned by hash | 02 | 04
row: Short-lived, scoped, provenance-backed publishing | 04
row: Hunt the persistence, not just the package | 05 | 06 | 07
row: Alert on security tooling fetched by a build | 03
row: Rotate everything a developer machine could reach | 03
At stage 01, MFA answers the phishing route, one disputed account of the entry.
notes:
This matrix sets the page's six defenses against its seven stages. A cell is marked only where a defense's own words name something that stage has.

Phishing-resistant MFA breaks the phishing route at step one, so it marks stage 01. Disabling install scripts answers stage 02, since everything the payload did began with a postinstall hook, and with enforced lockfile hashes, stage 04's force-published versions cannot silently enter your build. Short-lived, scoped publishing answers stage 04: the page says the worm propagated because a stolen token was a standing licence to publish anything the account owned.

Hunting the persistence names stage 05's workflow file and toJSON(secrets), stage 06's public Shai-Hulud repositories, and stage 07's discussion.yaml and self-hosted runners from the 2.0 wave. Alerting on security tooling in a build names stage 03's TruffleHog fetch. Rotating everything a developer machine could reach answers stage 03's haul. Its audit-log check names repositories you did not create, and stage 06 created one, but the page gives the late-August s1ngularity second wave as the reason for that check, so that cell stays empty.

At stage 01, the MFA defense answers the phishing route Unit 42 suggested. The page does not say which account of the entry is right. That is the second discussion question.

== Phishing-resistant MFA, and no install scripts
layout: compare
col: Phishing-resistant MFA (01) | Breaks the phishing route Unit 42 suggested | FIDO2 keys and passkeys are bound to the origin | A lookalike domain cannot relay them | Registry-level enforcement for high-download packages
col: Install scripts and hashes (02, 04) | npm install --ignore-scripts in CI and on developer machines | Enforced lockfile hashes: a force-published version cannot silently enter your build | Skip anything published in the last 24 to 48 hours
The page says that delay would have skipped most of this worm's usable window.
notes:
Two defenses, one for the way in and one for the install hook.

First, phishing-resistant MFA on maintainer accounts, which the page says breaks the phishing route at step one. Where the entry is a relayed one-time code, as Unit 42 suggested here, FIDO2 keys and passkeys are bound to the origin, so a lookalike domain cannot relay them no matter how convincing the pretext. For anyone maintaining a package others depend on, the page calls this the step with the biggest payoff for the effort, and calls registry-level enforcement for high-download packages the ecosystem version of the same argument.

Second, disable install scripts and pin dependencies by hash. Everything the payload did began with a postinstall hook, so npm install --ignore-scripts in CI and on developer machines neutralizes it without knowing anything about this specific worm. Pair it with a lockfile whose integrity hashes are enforced, so a force-published version cannot silently enter your build. A lockfile records the exact version and hash of every dependency. Add a delay policy: not installing anything published in the last 24 to 48 hours, the page says, would have skipped most of this worm's usable window.

== Replace publish tokens; alert on scanners in builds
layout: compare
col: Short-lived publishing (04) | npm Trusted Publishing with OIDC removes the stealable object entirely | SLSA provenance ties a release to a specific workflow run | Granular tokens scoped to one package cap a theft's reach | Revoke tokens that bypass 2FA
col: Security tooling in builds (03) | Alert when a build fetches and runs a secret scanner | Treat unexpected ingress of any security binary as suspicious | Default-deny egress from build runners, so the fetch fails
The page: a stolen token was a standing licence to publish anything the account owned.
notes:
Two more defenses: one for the token the worm spread with, one for the tool it harvested with.

First, replace long-lived publish tokens with short-lived, scoped, provenance-backed publishing. The page says the worm propagated because a stolen token was a standing licence to publish anything the account owned. npm Trusted Publishing with OIDC removes the stealable object entirely; it lets a CI workflow publish without a long-lived token. SLSA provenance ties a release to a specific workflow run, and granular tokens scoped to one package cap what any single theft can reach. Revoke tokens that bypass 2FA, two-factor authentication.

Second, alert on your own security tooling being fetched and run by a build. A postinstall script downloading and executing TruffleHog is a strong signal precisely because the tool is legitimate: nothing about your dependency install should be pulling a secret scanner off the internet. More generally, the page says, treat unexpected ingress of any security binary into a build environment as suspicious, and default-deny egress from build runners so the fetch fails in the first place. Default-deny means blocking every destination not explicitly allowed.

== Hunt the persistence; rotate everything a machine could reach
layout: compare
col: Hunt the persistence (05, 06, 07) | shai-hulud-workflow.yml, and toJSON(secrets) in any workflow | Public repos named Shai-Hulud or Sha1-Hulud, in your org and developers' accounts | For the 2.0 wave: discussion.yaml and unexpected self-hosted runners
col: Rotate on reach (03) | Every credential on or reachable from an affected machine | The haul spanned npm, GitHub, all three major clouds and SSH keys | Audit logs: repos you did not create, private repos changing visibility, as in s1ngularity
The page: downgrading a package removes none of the persistence.
notes:
The last two defenses are about clean-up beyond the package itself.

Hunt the persistence, not just the package. Search every repository for .github/workflows/shai-hulud-workflow.yml and for any workflow containing toJSON(secrets), and look for public repositories named Shai-Hulud or Sha1-Hulud in your organization and in your developers' personal accounts. For the 2.0 wave, check for discussion.yaml and for unexpected self-hosted runners registered against your repositories. Downgrading a package removes none of these.

Also assume anything a developer machine could reach is compromised, and rotate on that basis. The haul spanned npm, GitHub, all three major clouds and SSH keys, so the response scope is every credential present on or reachable from an affected machine, not just the npm token. The page also says to check GitHub audit logs for repositories you did not create and for private repositories that changed visibility, because that is how the late-August s1ngularity second wave played out.

== Traces the page names, and where they show up
layout: table
row: Where | What the page names
row: Package | Size jumping an order of magnitude between patch versions
row: Install | A build or install process fetching and running TruffleHog
row: Repository | shai-hulud-workflow.yml; toJSON(secrets) in any workflow
row: GitHub account | Public repos named Shai-Hulud or Sha1-Hulud
row: Network | A webhook.site endpoint, in some variants
row: 2.0 wave | discussion.yaml; unexpected self-hosted runners
The page calls the size check cheap and high-signal, and says almost nobody runs it.
notes:
These are the traces the page names, gathered in one place.

In the package itself, stage 02 points to size: a sudden order-of-magnitude jump between patch versions, which the page calls a cheap, high-signal check that almost nobody runs. At install time, stage 03's detection angle is the unexpected download and execution of security tooling, here TruffleHog, by a build or install process.

In repositories, look for .github/workflows/shai-hulud-workflow.yml and for toJSON(secrets) in any workflow. In GitHub accounts, look for public repositories named Shai-Hulud, or Sha1-Hulud from the 2.0 wave. Some variants also sent data to a webhook.site endpoint. For the 2.0 wave, look for discussion.yaml and for self-hosted runners you did not expect.

The page's caveat for the repository traces: downgrading a package removes none of them.

== Check your own environment
layout: checklist
check: Do your npm maintainers sign in with FIDO2 keys or passkeys?
check: Do CI and developer machines install with --ignore-scripts?
check: Are lockfile integrity hashes enforced, with a delay on new versions?
check: Do you publish through Trusted Publishing, or with long-lived tokens?
check: Would a build that fetches a secret scanner raise an alert?
check: Can you search every repository for toJSON(secrets) in a workflow?
check: Could you list every credential one developer machine can reach?
notes:
Turn the page's defenses into questions about your own estate. Each question comes from one of them.

Ask whether your maintainers use FIDO2 keys or passkeys, which a lookalike domain cannot relay. Ask whether CI and developer machines install with --ignore-scripts, whether lockfile integrity hashes are enforced, and whether anything published in the last 24 to 48 hours is held back.

If you publish packages, ask whether you use Trusted Publishing or still hold long-lived tokens, and whether any token bypasses 2FA.

Ask whether a build that pulled a secret scanner off the internet would raise an alert, and whether build runners can reach any destination they like.

Finally, ask whether you could search every repository for toJSON(secrets) and shai-hulud-workflow.yml today, and whether you could list every credential present on or reachable from one developer machine.

== The package is downgraded. What is still running?
layout: question
Your team has removed the compromised versions from every build. The page says any response that stopped there left something running.

What is it, and where would you look?
notes:
Give the room a minute with this one before moving on.

The setting is stages 05 to 07. The page says removing the malicious dependency does nothing to a workflow file sitting in the repository, and that any response that stopped at downgrading the package left it running.

Ask each person to name what they would look for, and where, before you show the answer slide. Watch for answers that stop at the npm side.

== Hunt the persistence, and rotate on reach
layout: answer
The page: downgrading a package removes none of the persistence. Hunt for it, and rotate.
- shai-hulud-workflow.yml, firing on every push
- Public Shai-Hulud repos holding the secrets
- For 2.0: discussion.yaml and self-hosted runners
- Rotate every credential the machine could reach
notes:
The page's answer is to hunt the persistence, not just the package.

The planted workflow, .github/workflows/shai-hulud-workflow.yml, keeps firing on every push by whoever commits next, so search every repository for it and for toJSON(secrets) in any workflow. Look for public repositories named Shai-Hulud or Sha1-Hulud in your organization and your developers' personal accounts. For the 2.0 wave, check for discussion.yaml and for unexpected self-hosted runners.

Rotate as well. The page says to assume anything a developer machine could reach is compromised. The haul spanned npm, GitHub, all three major clouds and SSH keys, so the scope is every credential on or reachable from an affected machine, not just the npm token.

Bring the group's answers back to this. Anyone who stopped at the package left the workflow running.

== Three accounts of the entry. Which defenses depend on them?
layout: question
Unit 42 suggested phishing. The tinycolor maintainer wrote that his token came from a GitHub Actions secret. Wiz assessed it as directly downstream of s1ngularity/Nx.

Which of the page's defenses would you apply without knowing who is right?
notes:
This question comes from stage 01, where the page sets out the accounts without choosing between them.

Unit 42 said the attack may have started with phishing that sent maintainers to fake MFA update pages. The @ctrl/tinycolor maintainer wrote that no phishing was involved and that his npm token was taken from a GitHub Actions secret in a shared repository. Wiz assessed the campaign as directly downstream of the late-August s1ngularity/Nx compromise. CISA's alert names no initial access vector.

Let the group sort the page's six defenses before you show the answer slide. Ask which ones they would put in place even if the entry is never settled.

== MFA answers the phishing route; the rest answer later stages
layout: answer
The page does not say which account is right. It ties phishing-resistant MFA to the phishing route at stage 01. Each of its other five defenses is tied to a later stage, from the install hook to the persistence.
notes:
The page's answer is in where it ties its defenses.

Phishing-resistant MFA breaks the phishing route at step one, where the entry is a relayed one-time code, as Unit 42 suggested. The page does not say which account of the entry is right.

The other five defenses are tied to later stages: install scripts and hashes to stages 02 and 04, short-lived publishing to stage 04, alerts on security tooling and rotation to stage 03, and hunting the persistence to stages 05 to 07.

The group may argue that Trusted Publishing reaches stage 01 as well, since every account agrees the target was a maintainer's npm publishing token, and the page says Trusted Publishing removes the stealable object entirely. The page ties that defense to propagation at stage 04, not to the entry, so that argument is the group's own.

